cartridge "DON'T CRY WOLF/CZES KOSNIOWSKI/1983"

' DON'T CRY WOLF - ColecoVision version of the Oric-1 game by Czes Kosniowski
' ("Games to Play on Your Oric-1", 1983, pp. 7-12). Adapted by Amy & Claude,
' Amy Studio 2026. Built by build.py from this file, ../_common/amy/core.alexis,
' pages.txt, art.py and music/*.txt.
'
' THE GAME (as in the book): you are lost in a forest (25% trees) and must
' walk home to your cottage door. Wolves start hidden. Each of your steps is
' a turn: with a 30% chance the next hidden wolf shows itself (and no wolf
' moves), otherwise every wolf, seen or not, steps towards you, trying the
' book's 8 alternatives (tables U, V) when its way is blocked. Wolves cannot
' squeeze diagonally between two trees; you can. A seen wolf that steps onto
' you catches you. Reaching the door starts a new forest with one more wolf
' (at most 20).
'
' CONTROLS: joystick, 8 ways = one step. Either button = stay put for one
' turn (the wolves move). Title: up/down choose, left/right change the
' number of wolves (2-9, as the book asks), a button confirms.
' v3 CONTROLS (same turns, same rules; see sub Controls):
' - settle: a push on one axis waits up to 3 frames for the second axis, so
'   a diagonal registers even when the two contacts close on different
'   frames; a diagonal is taken at once. A tap shorter than that still
'   counts when let go, except what is left of a diagonal being released.
' - a frame (sprite 4) shows the cell the stick points to: white = free,
'   red = a tree, the forest edge or a wolf (no turn is used there).
' - holding repeats the step after 14 frames, then every 8, but never when
'   a seen wolf is 2 cells away or less, and not after a wolf has appeared
'   or a bump, until the stick is let go.
' - one input (step or stay) made while the walker or the wolves are still
'   moving is kept and played next; older than 20 frames, or made before a
'   wolf appeared, it is dropped. A new forest waits for the stick and the
'   buttons to be let go.
' - bumping into a tree: a thud and a 2-pixel nudge, no turn used (as v1).
'
' LIBERTIES (listed for Amy):
' - Play field 26 x 18 cells (the Oric's was 34 x 21): the ColecoVision safe
'   area (columns 2-29, rows 1-22) is smaller. Corners, start and home keep
'   the book's places relative to the frame; the "not near the start" rule for
'   wolves (B - A < 17) is scaled to row - col < 13.
' - Three lives (shown as icons) instead of one: being caught costs a life
'   and you try a new forest with the same wolves. The book's game ended.
' - Bumping into a tree or the frame does not use a turn (on the Oric any
'   key press did). The button gives the book's "waste a turn" on purpose.
' - A path home always exists: a random monotone trail from the start to the
'   door is kept free of trees (the book could wall you in).
' - Two wolves never start on the same cell.
' - The book's appear test read past the last wolf (A(WOLVES)); here a wolf
'   only appears while some are still hidden.
' - FAIR WOLVES (v2, after Amy's feedback; measured with model/wolfsim.c):
'   a hidden wolf only shows itself if it is at least 3 cells away from
'   the walker (Chebyshev), and not closer to the cottage door than the
'   walker by more than (level - 1) cells; the first hidden wolf that
'   qualifies is the one shown (none: the wolves move). A wolf that has
'   just shown itself is startled and skips its first move.
' - Colour, animation, music and sound effects are new; the Oric's two-note
'   howl, ZAP and two PINGs are kept as the cues.

